✅2.1 When we create a Runnable object, is a new thread created?

Example:
Runnable task = new MyRunnable();

Output:
No new thread is created

Why?
🔴 Runnable only represents the task (business logic).
🔴 It does not represent an actual thread.
🔴 Creating a Runnable object only allocates memory for the task.
🔴 No execution starts until a Thread object is created and start() is invoked.

At this stage:

Main Thread
      │
      │ creates Runnable object
      ▼
Runnable Object Created
      │
      ▼
No Thread Created
      │
      ▼
No Execution

A thread is created only when:

Thread thread = new Thread(task);
thread.start();